      "answer": "The search results already returned the full rulebook text for *Brittle*. No additional tool calls are needed — the definition is fully present in the retrieved passages. Here is the answer:\n\n---\n\n## Brittle — Frosthaven Negative Condition\n\n> **Brittle:** The next time the figure would suffer damage from any source, they instead suffer double that amount of damage, and Brittle is then removed. Brittle is also removed if the figure is healed.\n\n*(Frosthaven Rulebook, Negative Conditions)*\n\n---\n\n### Key mechanics at a glance\n\n- **Trigger:** Activates on the **next instance of damage** the figure suffers, from **any source** (attacks, hazardous terrain, abilities, etc.).\n- **Effect:** That damage is **doubled**, then *Brittle* is removed.\n- **Healing removal:** *Brittle* is removed immediately if the figure is *healed*, even if no damage has been taken yet.\n- **Ward interaction:** If a figure with both *Ward* and *Brittle* suffers damage, the two conditions **negate each other** and both are removed — the damage is neither doubled nor halved. *(Rulebook, Positive Conditions — Ward)*\n- **One-shot:** It is not a persistent effect. Once it triggers (or is healed away), it is gone.\n\n### Practical notes\n- Applying *Brittle* before a big hit is a strong combo — enemies hit for double on the very next damage instance.\n- Healing a *Brittled* ally before they take damage safely removes the condition with no downside.\n- Some monsters (e.g., Snow Imps) **start with** *Brittle* as a permanent stat note, meaning they are always vulnerable to doubled damage on the first hit each time it is applied.",
